1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,854
Hi Shriya.

2
00:00:00,854 --> 00:00:05,976
This is Payments Lab, the live demonstration of your containerised payments project.

3
00:00:05,976 --> 00:00:11,952
It shows how an online store can create orders, record payment results, and avoid

4
00:00:11,952 --> 00:00:14,940
processing the same payment more than once.

5
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:18,355
The website and database are real and online.

6
00:00:18,355 --> 00:00:24,331
The payments are simulated, so no bank is connected and no money is charged.

7
00:00:25,131 --> 00:00:27,314
There are three main parts.

8
00:00:27,314 --> 00:00:32,552
The dashboard is the frontend: it collects an amount and shows results.

9
00:00:32,552 --> 00:00:37,791
The Fast API backend checks the request and processes the simulated payment.

10
00:00:37,791 --> 00:00:41,720
Mongo D B Atlas saves the order and transaction.

11
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:46,522
Vercel hosts the website and runs the backend when requests arrive.

12
00:00:46,522 --> 00:00:52,633
This hosted edition uses one backend application; the original project has two independently deployed

13
00:00:52,633 --> 00:00:53,070
services.

14
00:00:53,870 --> 00:00:58,196
Let's create an order for forty two rupees and choose Successful.

15
00:00:58,196 --> 00:01:02,522
Clicking Create order sends a real request to the deployed backend.

16
00:01:02,522 --> 00:01:07,241
The order starts pending, then becomes paid when the simulated payment succeeds.

17
00:01:07,241 --> 00:01:11,960
The response contains an order I D and a transaction I D.

18
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:15,893
Notice that the stored amount is four thousand two hundred.

19
00:01:15,893 --> 00:01:20,219
That represents paise, which avoids using imprecise decimal numbers for money.

20
00:01:21,019 --> 00:01:23,544
Now I retrieve the saved order.

21
00:01:23,544 --> 00:01:28,595
The same order and transaction identifiers come back from Mongo D B.

22
00:01:28,595 --> 00:01:33,224
This is stored data, not a result invented by the dashboard.

23
00:01:33,224 --> 00:01:38,695
Your order history also survives a page refresh in the same browser session.

24
00:01:38,695 --> 00:01:44,588
The sandbox separates each browser session's records, and demo records expire after seven days.

25
00:01:44,588 --> 00:01:47,534
It does not have registered user accounts.

26
00:01:48,334 --> 00:01:51,682
Next I enter fifteen rupees and select Declined.

27
00:01:51,682 --> 00:01:54,193
This time, the order becomes failed.

28
00:01:54,193 --> 00:02:00,053
The backend still saves the result and transaction identifier, so we can tell what

29
00:02:00,053 --> 00:02:00,471
happened.

30
00:02:00,471 --> 00:02:04,238
A simulated decline is different from a network timeout.

31
00:02:04,238 --> 00:02:10,098
If the outcome is uncertain, an order can remain pending until it is safely

32
00:02:10,098 --> 00:02:14,283
recovered, rather than being incorrectly marked as a declined payment.

33
00:02:15,083 --> 00:02:17,650
This is the most important experiment.

34
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:23,640
I send three payment requests at the same time, with one shared idempotency key.

35
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:28,346
All three succeed, but they return exactly the same transaction identifier.

36
00:02:28,346 --> 00:02:32,624
A unique database index and an atomic operation prevent duplicates.

37
00:02:32,624 --> 00:02:36,475
Then I change the amount and reuse the key.

38
00:02:36,475 --> 00:02:40,753
The backend rejects that request with four oh nine Conflict.

39
00:02:40,753 --> 00:02:46,742
Repeating a payment request is safe; clicking Create order again creates a separate order.

40
00:02:47,542 --> 00:02:51,749
The original repository demonstrates the wider engineering system.

41
00:02:51,749 --> 00:02:55,955
Orders and Payments are separate Fast API services.

42
00:02:55,955 --> 00:02:57,532
Docker packages them.

43
00:02:57,532 --> 00:03:01,739
Kubernetes and Helm manage deployment, upgrades, and rollback.

44
00:03:01,739 --> 00:03:05,945
GitHub Actions runs automated checks and builds images.

45
00:03:05,945 --> 00:03:12,781
Prometheus collects response times and errors, Grafana displays them, and Alertmanager handles alerts.

46
00:03:12,781 --> 00:03:19,090
That local monitoring and Kubernetes stack is separate from this Vercel app.

47
00:03:19,090 --> 00:03:22,771
This video demonstrates the hosted payment flow.

48
00:03:23,571 --> 00:03:29,867
You can explain it like this: I built an order and payment simulation with

49
00:03:29,867 --> 00:03:35,264
persistent storage, duplicate transaction protection, failure recovery, automated testing, and deployment monitoring.

50
00:03:35,264 --> 00:03:41,561
To demonstrate it yourself, create a successful order, try a decline, retrieve the saved

51
00:03:41,561 --> 00:03:44,260
result, then run the retry experiment.

52
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:48,757
The app uses free hosting plans within their usage limits.

53
00:03:48,757 --> 00:03:54,154
It is a portfolio project, not a production system for real payments.
